Excluding order 54 with one hexagon through every edge
======================================================

Proposed composition, 18 October 2026. All individual premises and all
24 representative refutations have been root-adopted. This composition
awaits its own distinct review and root adoption. It runs no enumeration,
solver, formula construction or proof checker and changes no source ledger.

Statement and notation
----------------------

Theorem. There is no finite simple connected quartic graph on 54 vertices
of girth six in which every edge lies on exactly one simple hexagon.

Write Q for these hypotheses, without any bipartiteness assumption. For a
hexagon C of a hypothetical graph G, let t(C) be the number of vertices
at distance at least three from C. The acquired layer lemmas identify the
vertices at distance two with S (one parent in the first layer) and T
(two parents), and give |T|=|Z|=t(C), |S|=36-2t(C). These are actual distance
layers; no relaxed-model assignment is substituted for them.

1. The six-exterior case has a complete finite cover
---------------------------------------------------

Suppose t(C)=6. The accepted parent-cover theorem classifies the simple
six-vertex, six-edge auxiliary graph R, its proper opposite-anchor edge
colours, and all permitted identifications of actual first-layer parents.
Its fork and ear exclusions, eight remaining uncoloured shapes, twelve
colour rows and N/L/R parent choices give exactly 70 raw records. The
three letters refer to actual named parents, not just their anchor colours.
The cover allows disconnected R, leaves and an isolated Z; it imposes no
minimum degree two. In particular the K(2,3) plus isolated-vertex record is
included. It does not import the t=5 host-degree bound or t=4 local filters.

The acquired setup associates to every record a host on 54 vertices with
66 fixed edges and the entire permitted optional SS, SZ and ZZ domain.
Any G in that record is its fixed graph plus a subset of those optional
edges, after a permitted choice of names. Extra forbidden pairs are exactly
those justified by the accepted parent theorem. The S children reflect
actual parent deficits, so shared-parent records do not silently gain or
lose vertices. Isolated-Z possibilities, whose host degree can be 29, are
retained.

The acquired transport certificate gives, for each of the 70 raw domains,
a literal bijection of all 54 vertices to one of 24 representatives. Its
independent checker verified equality of the complete fixed-edge images
and of the complete optional-edge images, not merely degree sequences or
a canonical-label digest. Consequently it transports every completion in
the raw domain to a completion of that representative. Simplicity, degree,
girth and the number of hexagons through each edge are invariant under a
vertex bijection. No maximality or inequivalence of the 24 representatives
is needed. Their recorded covers are disjoint and their union is 0..69.

2. Each representative has a refuted necessary formula
------------------------------------------------------

For every representative, the acquired provider first constructs the
original degree-four and girth formula. Each of its twelve fixed T--Z
edges must lie on a hexagon in any Q completion. The complete catalogue
retains the literal host hexagons through those edges which can still
occur: only degree obstructions or individually validated shortcut
witnesses remove cycles. Shared positive gates are equivalences for the
conjunction of a cycle's optional edges, and each required edge supplies
one disjunction of those gates. A Q completion therefore extends its edge
assignment to all auxiliary variables and satisfies every such clause.

The universal shortcut clauses and all subsequently recorded lazy cuts
also have literal witnesses under the acquired predicate: a forbidden
short cycle, or distinct hexagons sharing an edge. Their negative edge
clauses are necessary for Q. The formula is a necessary condition only;
no converse satisfiability-to-Q assertion is used. The independent saved
readers recomputed the complete selected catalogue, checked its report
and digests, validated every witness in the entire cut journal, rebuilt
the whole DIMACS byte string in the acquired clause order, and required
exact equality with the proof's input formula.

The already reviewed fixed-original-unit active/history RUP kernel then
checked each complete proof and an explicit empty-clause conclusion. This
is an acquired computational proof boundary, not reliance on a solver's
UNSAT message. The precise dimensions and checked additions are::

