The companion composition JSON binds every row to its exact model,
formula, proof, returned checker result, receipt and root acceptance, and
lists its raw-domain cover. The four first representatives have separate
acceptances; the next nineteen share the nineteen-exclusions acceptance;
index 41 has its separate empty-clause closure acceptance. These are 24
literal representatives, not 24 newly counted graph isomorphism classes.

Index 0 uses the separately completed fixed-CNF proof after its original
integrated proof phase stopped. That original partial trace is retained.
For index 41 the first read rebuilt the exact necessary CNF and all 12777
witnesses but truthfully failed because the old trace had no explicit
empty-clause addition. A separate file preserves all 82448 old trace bytes
and appends only the two bytes ``0\n``. The unchanged acquired checker
accepted that file against the same 1170020-byte CNF: 1069 additions,
1068 deletions, and an original-root unit conflict. The root gate binds
the previously reconstructed CNF and the new full trace. No checker rule
was relaxed; the old failed trace, receipt and terminal record remain
unchanged. It is the successful separate checker result which supplies
this row's refutation.

Thus every representative's necessary formula is unsatisfiable. If a Q
graph had t(C)=6, the complete cover and a verified domain permutation
would place an isomorphic Q completion in one representative, inducing a
satisfying assignment of its refuted formula. This is a contradiction.
We have proved that no hexagon of a Q graph has t(C)=6.

3. Global contradiction
-----------------------

The acquired three- and four-exterior arguments imply t(C) in {0,5,6}
for every hexagon C. The separately acquired complete t=5 cover and its
three independently reconstructed RUP refutations exclude t(C)=5. Section
2 excludes t(C)=6. Hence every hexagon would have t(C)=0.

The acquired zero-neighbour theorem forbids this. More explicitly, G has
108 edges and each belongs to exactly one hexagon, so it has 18 hexagons.
The acquired linear-incidence theorem gives a simple six-regular graph H
on those hexagons. At a zero hexagon, two other zero hexagons cannot meet
it at opposite vertices. Its zero neighbours therefore number at most
three, and at least three of its H neighbours are positive. If n_0,n_+
count zero and positive hexagons, then 3n_0 <= 6n_+ and n_0+n_+=18, so
n_+>=6. This contradicts the conclusion that all t(C) are zero. QED.

The zero-neighbour theorem is used to exclude the all-zero case, not to
assert that an individual t=0 hexagon is impossible on its own. The proof
chooses no preferred hexagon and applies to both bipartite and
nonbipartite Q graphs.

Scope, reproducibility and attribution
--------------------------------------

The theorem settles the order-54 nonexistence obligation for parameters
(k,g,lambda)=(4,6,1). It does not alone assert the source's lower bound 57:
that conclusion also needs the previously acquired lower-order exclusion
and the divisibility join. It does not assert an exact extremal count,
exclude order 57, or give whole-original F012 credit. Algorithm completeness,
source statement concordance and public reproduction are separate intake
obligations, recorded in the source-scope follow-up rather than inferred
from this theorem.

This is a computer-assisted composition of the retained mathematical
lemmas, complete graph-to-CNF reductions and exact acquired certificate
checks. It is not a Lean formalization or a claim of historical novelty.
The authorship and borrowed premises recorded by the constituent proof,
source, review and acceptance files remain in force. The solver is a
certificate producer; its correctness is not an assumption of the
mathematical implication. The native RUP kernel's accepted semantics and
the independently read formula/witness implementation remain explicit
computational premises.

All proof traces, source code, input models, cut journals, literal reports,
acceptances and failed/partial historical episodes are preserved. In
particular the 98 MB and 85 MB refutations are mathematical evidence, not
build cache. This composition creates only this proof and a finite metadata
join. It performs no scientific replay, changes no public files or CURRENT,
and assigns no source credit. Exact premise paths and full SHA-256 identities
are in ``hexagon_ball_order54_composition.json`` beside this note.
